One of the things that makes InRoom worthwhile is that it can be used across not only
Dray but several other classes. Before continuing to develop InRoom, let's take a short
detour to create a Skeletos enemy that will enable you to see InRoom and later scripts
work across multiple kinds of GameObjects.
Enemy: Skeletos
We'll start by making a basic enemy for Dray, the Skeletos. Like the Stalfos in The Legend
of Zelda, Skeletos randomly wander around the dungeon room that they're in. Skeletos
can pass through other Skeletos and will eventually be able to damage Dray with a touch.
Skeletos Animation
Start by using two of the sprites you imported to make the Skeletos animation:
1. Under Project Pane > Art by Andrew Dennis > Skeletos, select both the Skeletos_0 and
Skeletos_1 sprites.
2. Drag these two sprites together into the Hierarchy. This creates a new Animation that
you should name Skeletos.anim and save in the _Animations folder.
3. Rename the Skeletos_0 GameObject in the Hierarchy to Skeletos.
4. At the top of the Inspector for the Skeletos GameObject, set the Layer to Enemies.
5. Set the Transform Position of Skeletos to P:[ 19, 7, 0 ]. You should now see it on
screen in the Game pane.
6. In the Sprite Renderer component of the Skeletos Inspector, set Sorting Layer to
Enemies.
